\DOC del_simp

\TYPE {del_simp : thm -> unit}

\SYNOPSIS
Take a theorem out of the simp set.

\DESCRIBE
A call {del_simp th} removes from the simp set every theorem that is
{th}, compared as {equals_thm} compares -- the same hypotheses and the
same conclusion. A term net does not forget, so the net is built again
from the theorems that are left, which costs as much as declaring all of
them did.

It is here for the rule that turned out to loop or to rewrite the wrong
way, not for everyday use.

\FAILURE
Never fails; removes nothing if no declared theorem is {th}.

\EXAMPLE
{
  # length (simps ());;
  val it : int = 6

  # del_simp (distinctness "exp");;
  val it : unit = ()

  # length (simps ());;
  val it : int = 5
}

\SEEALSO
add_simp, add_simps, equals_thm, simps.

\ENDDOC
